<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>More Cases</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide more cases of the evaluation process for GPT-5, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Qwen2.5VL-72B (SFT), and CoSMo-R1. We selected three representative problems from the MM-SafetyBench and SIUO Benchmarks to investigate the specific errors each model makes when faced with dangerous problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.2" class="ltx_logical-block">
        
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">See pages - of <a href="https://pics/case1.pdf" title="" class="ltx_ref">pics/case1.pdf</a>See pages - of <a href="https://pics/case2.pdf" title="" class="ltx_ref">pics/case2.pdf</a>See pages - of <a href="https://pics/case3.pdf" title="" class="ltx_ref">pics/case3.pdf</a></p>
</div>
        
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>More Cases on MM-SafetyBench and SIUO BenchMark.</figcaption>
</figure>
      
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle AOB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle AOB</annotation></semantics></math> is right-angled at <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, so has area <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(AO)(OB)=\frac{1}{2}a(1)=\frac{1}{2}a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(AO)(OB)=\frac{1}{2}a(1)=\frac{1}{2}a</annotation></semantics></math>.We next need to calculate the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle BCD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle BCD</annotation></semantics></math>.Method 1: Completing the trapezoid Drop a perpendicular from <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(3,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis. Then <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DOPC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DOPC</annotation></semantics></math> is a trapezoid with parallel sides <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DO</annotation></semantics></math> of length 1 and <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PC</annotation></semantics></math> of length 2 and height <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OP</annotation></semantics></math> (which is indeed perpendicular to the parallel sides) of length 3.The area of the trapezoid is thus <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(DO+PC)(OP)=\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(3)=\frac{9}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(DO+PC)(OP)=\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(3)=\frac{9}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.But the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle BCD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle BCD</annotation></semantics></math> equals the area of trapezoid <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DOPC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DOPC</annotation></semantics></math> minus the areas of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DOB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DOB</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle BPC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle BPC</annotation></semantics></math>.<math id="A1.SS5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DOB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DOB</annotation></semantics></math> is right-angled at <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, so has area <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(DO)(OB)=\frac{1}{2}(1)(1)=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(DO)(OB)=\frac{1}{2}(1)(1)=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.<math id="A1.SS5.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle BPC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle BPC</annotation></semantics></math> is right-angled at <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, so has area <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(BP)(PC)=\frac{1}{2}(2)(2)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(BP)(PC)=\frac{1}{2}(2)(2)=2</annotation></semantics></math>.Thus, the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DBC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DBC</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{9}{2}-\frac{1}{2}-2=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{9}{2}-\frac{1}{2}-2=2</annotation></semantics></math>.(A similar method for calculating the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DBC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DBC</annotation></semantics></math> would be to drop a perpendicular to <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-axis, creating a rectangle <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QOPC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QOPC</annotation></semantics></math>.) Method 2: <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DBC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DBC</annotation></semantics></math> is right-angled The slope of line segment <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DB</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-0}{0-1}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-0}{0-1}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>.The slope of line segment <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BC</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2-0}{3-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2-0}{3-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.Since the product of these slopes is -1 (that is, their slopes are negative reciprocals), then <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DB</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BC</annotation></semantics></math> are perpendicular.Therefore, the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DBC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DBC</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(DB)(BC)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(DB)(BC)</annotation></semantics></math>.Now <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DB=\sqrt{(1-0)^{2}+(0-1)^{2}}=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DB=\sqrt{(1-0)^{2}+(0-1)^{2}}=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BC=\sqrt{(3-1)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}}=\sqrt{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BC=\sqrt{(3-1)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}}=\sqrt{8}</annotation></semantics></math>.Thus, the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DBC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DBC</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{2}\sqrt{8}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{2}\sqrt{8}=2</annotation></semantics></math>.Since the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle AOB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle AOB</annotation></semantics></math> equals the area of <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle DBC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle DBC</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}a=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}a=2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
